eational boat and yacht retailer, marina operator, and superyacht services company in the United States. It operates through two segments, Retail Operations and Product Manufacturing. The company sells new and used recreational boats, including pleasure and fishing boats, mega-yachts, yachts, sport cruisers, motor yachts, yacht tenders, pontoon boats, ski boats, jet boats, and other recreational boats. It also offers marine parts and accessories comprising marine electronics; dock and anchoring products that include boat fenders, lines, and anchors; boat covers; trailer parts; water sport accessories, which comprise tubes, lines, wakeboards, and skis; engine parts; oils; lubricants; steering and control systems; corrosion control products and service products; high-performance accessories, including propellers and instruments; and a line of boating accessories, such as life jackets, inflatables, and water sports equipment. In addition, the company provides novelty items, such as shirts, caps, and license plates; marine engines and equipment; maintenance, repair, and slip and storage services; and boat or yacht brokerage services, as well as yacht charters and power catamarans. Further, it offers new or used boat finance services; arranges insurance coverage, including boat property, disability, undercoating, gel sealant, fabric protection, trailer tire and wheel protection, and casualty insurance coverage; and manufactures and sells sport yachts and yachts. Additionally, the company operates MarineMax vacations in Tortola and British Virgin Islands. It also markets and sells its products through offsite locations and print catalog. The company was incorporated in 1998 and is based in Oldsmar, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. William Brett McGill | CEO, President & Director | Age 57 | Compensation: $1.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $653K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $34K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["MarineMax, Inc.  (FL) is a small-cap consumer cyclical business with a $1.2B market cap on $2.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +9% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 5% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 26%.","The business throws off $114M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 8.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 0.9\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 6.2% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 39% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.39298861701055876},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.2% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Consumer Cyclical peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Consumer Cyclical peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Consumer Cyclical sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 8% over 3 months"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Trading Systems Reacting to (HZO) Volatility","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/23/Trading_Systems_Reacting_to_HZO_Volatility_112126070201_1795305721.html","date":"2026-11-22","timestamp":1795305721.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"MarineMax EVP Shawn Berg Awarded 3,048 RSUs on November 16, 2026","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/marinemax-evp-shawn-berg-awarded-3048-rsus-on-november-16-2026","date":"2026-11-18","timestamp":1795039105.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.402,"tier":1,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"MarineMax Director George E. Borst Reports Acquisition of 2,651 RSUs on November 16, 2026","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/marinemax-director-george-e-borst-reports-acquisition-of-2651-rsus-on-november-16-2026","date":"2026-11-18","timesta